of the stanza. As the vowel changes from *a* in *father*, to *a* in *mat*; *e* in *met*. or *i* in *sit*, or again to *o* in *not*, *u* in *but*, and *u* in *put*, the musical result is totally different, though the pitch remains unaltered.
>>>
<<<
pitch remains unaltered.
===
pitch remains unaltered. To shew the effect of the different vowels throughout a piece of music, I asked a set of about 8 voices to sing, before about 200 others, the first half of *See the conquering hero comes*, first to *lah*, then to *lee*, and then to *loo*. The difference of effect was almost ludicrous.
>>>
<<<
almost ludicrous.
===
almost ludicrous. Much has to be studied in the relation of the qualities of vowels to the effect of the music. In this respect, too, the *pitch* chosen for the tonic will be found of great importance.—*Translator*.]
>>>
<<<
great importance.—*Translator*.]
===
great importance.—*Translator*.]

Footnote: † [This refers to Germany, not to the English Tonic Solfaists, nor to the English madrigal singers. On Dec. 27, 1869, at a meeting of the Tonic Solfa College I had an unusual opportunity of contrasting the effect of just and tempered intonation in the singing of the same choir.
>>>
<<<
the same choir.
===
the same choir. It was a choir of about 60 mixed voices, which had gained the prize at the International Exhibition at Paris in 1867, and had been kept well together ever since. After ¶ singing some pieces without accompaniment, and hence in the just intonation to which the singers had been trained,
>>>
<<<
had been trained,
===
had been trained, and with the most delightful effect of harmony, they sang a piece with a pianoforte accompaniment. Of course the pianoforte itself was inaudible among the mass of sound produced by sixty voices. But it had the effect of perverting their intonation, and the whole charm of the singing was at once destroyed.
>>>
<<<
at once destroyed.
===
at once destroyed. There was nothing left but the everyday singing of an ordinary choir. The disillusion was complete and the effect most unsatisfactory as a conclusion. If the same piece of music or succession of chords in *C* major or *C* minor, without any modulation,
>>>
<<<
without any modulation,
===
without any modulation, be played first on the Harmonical and then be contrasted with an ordinary tempered harmonium, the same kind of difference will be felt, but not so strongly.—*Translator*.]
>>>
